<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Where homeowner estimates usually fall short</h2>
<p>We rarely see a carrier estimate that is wrong everywhere. We often see one that is wrong in five or six expensive places. On Kansas homes those places tend to repeat:</p>
<ul>
<li>Only the “worst” roof slopes approved when hail hit the whole roof.</li>
<li>Siding repaired on one elevation when the discontinued profile can’t be matched.</li>
<li>Gutters, downspouts, window screens, AC fins and garage doors left off a hail claim.</li>
<li>Basement water losses written as a carpet-dry job when drywall, trim and insulation are wet.</li>
<li>Detached garages, sheds and fences missed under Other Structures.</li>
<li>Code items such as drip edge, ice-and-water shield or decking replacement priced at zero.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How a homeowner appraisal runs</h2>
<p>We start with a review of your declarations page, the carrier’s full estimate and your photos. If appraisal looks worthwhile, we inspect, build our own estimate and, once appraisal is demanded, serve as your appointed appraiser. You get a plain-English comparison showing every line where we disagree with the carrier and why.</p>
<h2>Housing we work on every week</h2>
<p>Post-war ranches in Wichita and Topeka, split-levels in Overland Park and Shawnee, 1920s brick bungalows in Kansas City, limestone farmhouses in the Smoky Hills, new-build subdivisions in Olathe and Andover, and manufactured homes on rural acreages. Each one prices differently, and a statewide price list rarely captures that.</p>
<h2>Contents and additional living expense</h2>
<p>If the loss forced you out of the house, additional living expense and contents inventories can be as contested as the building itself. We help organize inventories with age, condition and replacement pricing so they can be valued on the same footing as the structure.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary><span>My insurer paid something already. Can I still use appraisal?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Usually yes. Payment of the undisputed amount doesn’t end your right to dispute the rest, though deadlines in your policy still apply.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Do you work on condo units?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Condo claims often involve both the association’s master policy and the owner’s HO-6 policy. We help sort which policy covers which part of the loss.</p></div></details><details><summary><span>Will appraisal raise my premium?</span><svg class="pl" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14"/></svg></summary><div class="a"><p>We can’t predict underwriting decisions. Appraisal is a policy condition, not a new claim, but you should ask your agent about renewal practices.</p></div></details></div>
